  "limitations": [
    "Limited Scope: The experiment exclusively investigates the restorative effects of different autumn landscapes in a virtual environment, while the effects of real-world landscapes may be influenced by seasonal and regional variations.",
    "Further Exploration of Other Sensory Dimensions: While this study primarily focuses on visual and auditory perception, future investigations could delve into other sensory aspects such as olfaction and tactile sensation.",
    "Subtle differences might exist between the visual and auditory groups in terms of material collection for experiments, laboratory setups, and testing methods.",
    "Regional and Seasonal Variations: The materials for this experiment were gathered on a campus setting, and variations in landscapes and plant species may differ significantly in various regions and seasons.",
    "Experiment Duration Limitation: The stimulation duration in the virtual environment was only three minutes, which may affect the accuracy of the results concerning restorative effects.",
    "Comparison between Real and Virtual Environments: This study did not extensively explore the differential sensory effects in real and virtual environments.",
    "Stress tests: The study involved stress tests to enhance performance but did not conduct control tests without stress to evaluate performance differences."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated the impact of virtual autumn color and bare tree landscapes, presented through visual, auditory, and combined sensory modes, on the physiological (EEG, heart rate) and psychological (mood, affect, recovery, vitality) well-being of 320 university students. Findings indicate that autumn color landscapes generally had a superior therapeutic effect, increasing relaxation and positive mood, and that bare tree landscapes also offered restorative benefits, particularly in visual mode. Different sensory modes yielded varying therapeutic outcomes, with auditory stimulation often showing pronounced psychological changes, while audio-visual combinations sometimes showed mixed or non-significant effects despite general multisensory benefits. Limitations include the virtual environment setting, short stimulation duration, and lack of other sensory dimensions."
}